welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn wilshire host and our guest is  Elizabeth Bradley the faculty director  for the Global Health Initiative and the  global health Leadership Institute both  of which are here at Yale she is also a  professor at yale school of public  health professor Bradley's research  focuses on health delivery systems and  quality improvement and has contributed  important findings about organizational  change and quality of care within the  hospital nursing home and hospice  settings she has been involved with  several projects that aim to strengthen  health systems in international settings  including China Ethiopia Liberia South  Africa and the United Kingdom today we  talk with Professor Bradley about the  global health efforts at Yale as well as  some of her recent work as a recipient  of a Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation  grant welcome professor Bradley thank  you so much a great honor to be here  well thank you let's begin with talking  about the global health initiatives and  how they've evolved over the past three  years here at Yale sure thank you very  much it was in 2009 when President leben  and president-elect Sal of a recognized  that students at Yale were dying to do  more in the area of health promotion  globally and we really didn't have any  programs in that we had individual  courses we had individual faculty but  they decided we needed to coalesce the  resources that we had at Yale with in  something called the Global Health  Initiative within very and they asked me  to direct this initiative it is a  faculty-led initiative we have an  advisory group of faculty so that we try  very hard to make it interdisciplinary  from the very beginning the initiative  has ended up generating enormous  interest I think more than 30 or 35  courses have been now started in the  global health area we have an  undergraduate Global Health fellowship  and Global Health Studies program in the  graduate school we also have a global  health certificate now and finally in  the professional school  of nursing medicine and public health  they all have global health  concentrations now so it's a wholly  different than three years ago and what  does the future hold you think for the  Global Health Initiative and how are you  going to get there yeah I think the  future is very bright we have more and  more students taking these classes we  have more and more faculty who are  meeting each other this is another  enormous contribution of doing something  as a single University we can be more  unified in our approach for instance  before 2009 somebody in anthropology  might be working in Nepal and have no  idea that an economist is working there  also and if they could collaborate they  could really make change so because we  have more educational programs because  the faculty are meeting each other from  different disciplines I think it's very  very bright a couple of the things that  we absolutely need we know from our past  work is we have to have championship at  the top of the university and we have  that very strongly I think with Peter  Salome and we also have to have the  champions that are in the donor pools  that are really thinking how can a  university improve human health in the  globe and so far I think the donors that  we have it you'll have been very  generous but I think that's important to  continue to grow attract new faculty  attract some of the greatest minds in  this to Yale can you speak to any of  these successes that you've had to date  out in the world making specific impacts  and countries sure well I think my  favorite story is the one from Ghana and  this is because Ghana really had been a  country in which Yale was working with  very closely on a number of levels Linda  lorimer has visited you know it's a  country that we know the president of  etc and we ended up doing a huge  research project there Chris Udrih  really red LED this from the economics  department to understand what really is  the employment impact of mental health  problems in Ghana so this is a situation  where epidemiologist came together  anthropologists economists from Ghana  and from Yale and ultimately created the  research necessary to quantify what is  the GDP lost that is attributable to  mental health problems in the  country and ultimately the GDP  equivalent of the mental health problems  were like six or seven percent of the  gdp for gone to be able to itemize that  and then t that information up to the  key policy people including the  President of Ghana made all the  difference in the last eight months  they've written their first mental  health policy that is a state-of-the-art  policy for improving mental health and  Ghana now there's a lot of action to be  done on the ground which I think we have  the staff to help them with but it's a  place where research and practice met  and now I think many many people in  Ghana can be better off are you  targeting another country to do the same  kind of work we tried hard to find out  the countries that we're working with  what is their priority the reason this  worked in Ghana is going to asked for  help on this issue so they were primed  to do something and so when we work with  other countries we try to start with  that too so it's not the same issue in  another country but Ethiopia would be  another place we work like this with but  in that country where we work much more  on the quality of their hospitals and  then we're also working with several  other countries on different sort of  public health problems they've brought  to us we try to figure out who are the  faculty you can do something I'm right  great do you also send students out into  the world to try and assist absolutely  tell us talk a little bit about some of  the experiences yeah the students have  just been unbelievably creative in what  they have come up with and we do through  the Global Health Initiative we probably  send 50 or 60 students ultimately go  overseas to do something in the area of  Health and some of the more  transformational things that have  occurred we had one student last year  who went to Ethiopia specifically and  worked on a new method for giving it's  giving birth in health centers in  Ethiopia ninety-three percent of women  give birth in their village which would  be in a hut with no really sanitation or  anything like this and the student who  was pre-med actually got involved in  redesigning a prototype ideal kind of  Health Center where they would have the  right kind of gloves they would have the  right kind of sort of cultural norms to  be able to bring women to give birth  there and it's really in place now  really and that student was you know  they were part of a team but be  because they could be wrapped in a large  team of people working on something like  this it could make a difference the  student on their own probably couldn't  do that much but when you wrap them with  a ongoing initiative like this they can  be placed in a place to really be  successful another success story I think  is the Global Leadership Institute yep  so let's talk a little bit about that  tell us about it and how it is  successful how it fits into the program  overall yeah actually the global health  Leadership Institute is the flagship  program of the Global Health Initiative  it is what began the Global Health  Initiative and I think it's important  that it's a Leadership Institute this is  what distinguishes Yale in its global  health work if we look at the mission of  Yale in addition to discovering new  knowledge it is to educate people for  leadership so in the global health arena  the Leadership Institute works on  courses that are helpful for students  but also engaging with other countries  delegations who want to lead to make a  change so every year we have a  conference that's a five-day conference  where we invite for delegations from  four separate countries very high-level  policy makers and we embed students in  those delegations and faculty so then  you have a group who's really able to go  back they they develop a strategic plan  to address the problem they want and  they go back to their country and do  that so what are some of the countries  well Ghana Ethiopia Liberia has come  South Africa has come Brazil and Turkey  are going to come this year okay yeah  it's all different and I think I'm the  Brazil and Turkey effort is going to be  on how to this will bring together  several things we've done because it  will be focused on children and it will  be a focus on children's mental health  development at very young ages how do  you bring about healthy development to  hopefully build a healthy adult group so  I think it's going to be fast and I  wonderful lastly let's talk about your  the work you've been doing with the  melinda and bill gates foundation and  specifically the aided model sure now  this is fascinating work bill and  melinda gates foundation was worried  about a problem they had which is they  constantly are discovering new  transformational  innovations they could use but then  people wouldn't necessarily take them up  so whereas we look at Microsoft how  successful it was when Melinda and Bill  and Melinda Gates might go to Nepal and  say here's a new way in which will do  contraception can you use this widget  they realize no one was really taking it  up so they contracted with us to develop  a model of dissemination and diffusion  how do you take a good idea and put it  into practice we ended up getting a  multidisciplinary team together and  doing a very intensive literature review  of all kinds of public health  innovations that had spread virally and  studied exactly how did it occur and in  fact we ended up using a model from  molecular biology to understand well how  does HIV spread what how does it work  and can we look at socially can we look  at analogues to each of those steps and  see it in the social world we ended up  creating a model called Eden which has  five steps to it all that happened kind  of in a complex adaptive system but  assessing the environment innovating to  fit really a receptor user group  developing support around the innovation  political and economic support engaging  in the user community and then devolving  to other users through social networks  and the model is fascinating because  pretty much everybody that we work with  now as we are trying to spread our new  information we might get we think of  wrapping it in this model it's sort of a  way you know marketing people might know  this second nature but global health  people this isn't something that we've  really used now when you talk about that  model what comes to mind is the person  who was in Africa helping with the  deliveries and I immediately thought I  culturally would this be something that  the women would embrace going to a  hospital versus the the centuries-old  practice of giving birth in their hot so  was that an obstacle to overcome Norma's  obstacle and the way it was overcome is  one very important piece of giving birth  in Ethiopia is the coffee ceremony and  so we ended up including a full coffee  ceremony  in the clinic where you would give birth  so there you have to tell me what the  coffee ceremony yes oh you think opiate  coffee is the best of all but it is a  very carefully done ceremony where  people are served coffee three cups of  coffee that you have incense and popcorn  and it's really a busy while the woman  is giving birth or are after yeah but  it's a celebration and it's something  Ethiopians know to do and it costs money  and so what we ended up doing was  designing a method by which you could  make it culturally acceptable to go to a  clinic where the people who brought you  would be fed and you'd have you know a  feast and a coffee ceremony afterwards  so little things like that I mean there  are many many more clinical things but  that's something maybe the lay person  can understand you're sort of making  something fit in the culture right right  I think it got that that cultural aspect  and possibly also an economic a specter  are the two biggest hurdles toward why  something would not be taken and adapted  and used the magic sure politics okay an  economic right yeah and so we often find  that the economic world might be working  against the health world at times and we  have to work carefully to say how can we  use what is reality and maybe twist it a  little bit so that we can have our cake  and eat it too we can have a healthy  economy and we can have healthy people  right very good thank you so much for  being here today in sharing some of your  work well thank you for your interest is  very very fun great for more information  about professor bradley and her research  please visit our website at yale edu  slash mcmillan report be sure to join us  again for another episode of the  macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale  you

